# Tracker mirror: Aura ↔ Jira (template)

> **This file is inert here.** It sits in the universal rule tree like any other rule, but `alwaysApply: false` and no `globs` keep it from ever attaching: nothing in this repo reads it while the target repo's `AGENTS.md` → key `Tracker` names a single tracker. Central home is `blueprint/rules/tracker-mirror.mdc` in the wiki (AURA-1647), published there with a condition naming more than one tracker — this local copy is what that condition, once true, installs. It is carried, not run — see `general-ai-docs-structure` → "Rule templates".
>
> **A repo adopting it needs all three:** both adapters (`tracker-aura` and `tracker-jira`), the Atlassian MCP server wired up, and a `Tracker` value naming both systems. Copying this file alone produces a rule that describes calls the agent cannot make.

Everything here exists **only** because two trackers are in play. Single-tracker mechanics belong in the adapters; if a section below would still be true with one tracker, it is in the wrong file.

## Which one is the source of truth

the target repo's `AGENTS.md` → key `Tracker` lists them in order; **the first named is the source of truth**, the rest are mirrors. Every lifecycle step creates and updates the primary tracker **first**, then mirrors the change outward. No separate primacy key exists.

## Level mapping

| Jira | Aura `level` |
|---|---|
| Epic | `EPIC` |
| Story (Delivery / Discovery / Optimization / Maintenance) | `STORY` |
| Sub-Task (slice) | `SUBTASK` |
| — (no Jira equivalent) | `SAGA` (manual/optional only) |

## Type mapping

| Jira issue type | Aura `type` | Note |
|---|---|---|
| Delivery Story | `FEATURE` | |
| Bug | `BUG` | |
| Optimization / Maintenance Story | `CHORE` | |
| Discovery Story | `DISCOVERY` | also set `workflow_variant: DISCOVERY` |
| — (idea capture, no issue yet) | `IDEA` | |

## Link before any content change

Order is not cosmetic here — it is what prevents a duplicate issue:

1. Create the issue on the mirror side **explicitly**, with the correct type and required fields. Do not rely on an automatic push: it cannot choose the issue type, cannot set team/estimate/end-state fields, and returns its key asynchronously.
2. **Link the two immediately** (`linkJiraIssueToTask`) — before any further title, description or level change on the primary task.
3. Only then perform further content updates, the status walk, artifact links and relation edges.

**Why step 2 cannot wait:** Aura pushes to Jira automatically when a task PATCH sets the level or changes title/description on a task that already has one. If such a PATCH runs before the link exists, that push does not know an issue was just created and makes a **second** one. Linking closes the window. Status-only PATCHes never trigger it and are always safe.

## Extra parent-resolution step

The adapter's chain (deep link → `human_key` → confirmed title match → create) gains one exact step in mirror mode: resolve the parent's **key on the other tracker** to its linked task before falling through to a title search. It belongs after the `human_key` step, because it is exact — and before the title search, because that one is a guess.

## Error policy in mirror mode

This is the one place where a tracker failure has somewhere to fall back to: if the mirror side fails, complete the operation on the primary side and carry on. Surface a visible warning naming what needs reconciling later.

That fallback exists **only** here. With a single tracker the failure classification in the adapter applies instead, and it is stricter — an identity failure stops the run.
